    "temperature": {
      "file": "instrument_air_dryer_temperature.glb",
      "title": "Heat drives the water off the desiccant",
      "description": "In the regenerating tower, ambient air from the blower is heated (≈ 150–200 °C) and passed down through the bed, opposite to the drying flow, so the wettest desiccant at the inlet end is heated last and the outlet end stays driest. The heat front moves through the bed, then the tower is cooled and repressurised before switching. Compared with heatless dryers (≈ 15 % purge of dried air), blower-purge dryers use little or no compressed air. Instrument air is typically dried to a pressure dew point of about −40 °C (ISO 8573-1 class 2).",
      "render": "normal",
      "annotations": []
    }
  },
